; ACCEPTANCE: a Win32 function that takes an ENUM builds, runs, and the callee
; dispatches on the value we passed.
;
; GetFileInformationByHandleEx takes FILE_INFO_BY_HANDLE_CLASS, an enum, and was
; refused as "struct by value": the survey's struct table took the name after
; ANY closing brace, so `typedef enum _X { ... } X;` put X in it. An enum is a C
; `int` under Win64 -- one register (win32enum-2026-09-11).
;
; TWO CALLS, because one could not tell a delivered enum from a dropped one.
;
;   class 0 (FileBasicInfo), no buffer   ->  FALSE, then 24  ERROR_BAD_LENGTH
;   class 9999, not a class at all       ->  FALSE, then 87  ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER
;
; Different errors for different enum values means the callee read the enum we
; passed. THE FIRST PREDICTION WAS WRONG and is kept: it said 6,
; ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE, for the NULL handle -- but the function validates the
; buffer length for the class BEFORE it looks at the handle, which is itself the
; host saying it read the class. The expectations above are what the host
; answered, recorded as observed rather than dressed as predicted.
;
; It needs `-checked` for the reason wide-call.oro does: win/fmt.print-int does
; arithmetic on its argument, and a host call's result has no declared range,
; so ADR 0019 refuses to prove it in the window rather than assume it.

(use win/WinBase as w)
(use windows/kernel32 as k)
(use x64 as x)
(use win/fmt as fmt)

(export main)

(def main ()
  (let ok (w.GetFileInformationByHandleEx 0 0 (x.null) 0)
    (seq (fmt.print-int ok)
         (fmt.print-int (k.GetLastError))
         (w.GetFileInformationByHandleEx 0 9999 (x.null) 0)
         (fmt.print-int (k.GetLastError)))))
